\import{base-macros}
\date{2026-05-12T19:34:14Z}
\tag{public}
\author{kellenkanarios}
\title{[[arpaci2018operating]]: Exercises}
\p{Worked homework for [Three Easy Pieces](arpaci2018operating), one chapter per chapter of the book. The conceptual material lives in the concept pages — [[FCHM]], [[JJ45]], [[JE53]] for the process abstraction; [[O1S9]], [[J6VZ]], [[YHJR]], [[9V3H]] for the process API; [[JA6Z]], [[VJ4F]], [[NEL8]] for the schedulers. Simulator questions are answered twice over: what I predicted before running anything, then what the tool actually printed. The prediction is the experiment, and the ones I got wrong are the entries worth rereading.}

\subtree[ostep-ch4]{
\title{The Abstraction: The Process}
\meta{number}{4}

\exercise{1}{CPU utilization of two compute-bound processes}{
  \p{Run \code{process-run.py} with the flags \code{-l 5:100,5:100}. What should the CPU utilization be?}
}{
  \prediction{Two processes of #{5} instructions each, every instruction using the CPU with probability one, so nothing ever blocks:
  \codeblock{plaintext}{\verb<<<|
Process 0
  cpu
  cpu
  cpu
  cpu
  cpu

Process 1
  cpu
  cpu
  cpu
  cpu
  cpu
<<<}
  Utilization should be 100\%. The first process runs to completion, then the second. Were both waiting on I/O this would not hold.}
  \p{Confirmed with \code{-c -p}: 100\% utilization.}
}

\exercise{2}{Completion time when one process blocks on I/O}{
  \p{Run \code{process-run.py} with the flags \code{-l 4:100,1:0}. How long does it take to complete both processes?}
}{
  \prediction{One process of #{4} CPU instructions and one of a single I/O instruction:
  \codeblock{plaintext}{\verb<<<|
Process 0
  cpu
  cpu
  cpu
  cpu

Process 1
  io
  io_done
<<<}
  It should take #{4 + \text{time of IO}}: the compute-bound process runs its four instructions first, and only then does the second process start and sit out the whole I/O.}
  \p{With \code{-c -p} it takes #{11} timesteps, which is indeed #{\approx 4 + \text{time of IO}}.}
}

\exercise{3}{Why the order of the processes matters}{
  \p{Switch the order: \code{./process-run.py -l 1:0,4:100}. What happens now? Does switching the order matter, and why?}
}{
  \prediction{Now the trace is
  \codeblock{plaintext}{\verb<<<|
Process 0
  io
  io_done

Process 1
  cpu
  cpu
  cpu
  cpu
<<<}
  which should take #{\max(5, 1 + \text{time IO})} depending on the \code{-L} flag. Order matters, because the CPU instructions can run while the non-blocking I/O is outstanding — which is the argument for scheduling at all.}
  \p{It takes #{7} timesteps: the I/O overlaps with the compute as expected.}
}

\exercise{4}{SWITCH_ON_END serializes an I/O-bound process against a CPU-bound one}{
  \p{The \code{-S} flag decides how the system reacts when a process issues I/O. Under \code{SWITCH_ON_END} the system does \em{not} switch away while a process is doing I/O; it waits until that process finishes entirely. What happens with one I/O-bound and one CPU-bound process — \code{-l 1:0,4:100 -c -S SWITCH_ON_END}?}
}{
  \p{The whole CPU-bound process is held hostage by the I/O:}
  \codeblock{plaintext}{\verb<<<|
Time        PID: 0        PID: 1           CPU           IOs
  1         RUN:io         READY             1
  2        BLOCKED         READY                           1
  3        BLOCKED         READY                           1
  4        BLOCKED         READY                           1
  5        BLOCKED         READY                           1
  6        BLOCKED         READY                           1
  7*   RUN:io_done         READY             1
  8           DONE       RUN:cpu             1
  9           DONE       RUN:cpu             1
 10           DONE       RUN:cpu             1
 11           DONE       RUN:cpu             1
<<<}
}

\exercise{5}{SWITCH_ON_IO overlaps the two}{
  \p{Run the same processes with switching set to happen whenever one is \code{WAITING} for I/O — \code{-l 1:0,4:100 -c -S SWITCH_ON_IO}. What happens now? Use \code{-c} and \code{-p} to confirm.}
}{
  \prediction{Timestep #{1} still runs \code{RUN:io}, but then \code{PID: 1} should occupy the next #{4} timesteps until it completes. After one more \code{BLOCKED} step we run \code{RUN:io_done} and both finish.}
  \p{Exactly that:}
  \codeblock{plaintext}{\verb<<<|
Time        PID: 0        PID: 1           CPU           IOs
  1         RUN:io         READY             1
  2        BLOCKED       RUN:cpu             1             1
  3        BLOCKED       RUN:cpu             1             1
  4        BLOCKED       RUN:cpu             1             1
  5        BLOCKED       RUN:cpu             1             1
  6        BLOCKED          DONE                           1
  7*   RUN:io_done          DONE             1
<<<}
}

\exercise{6}{IO_RUN_LATER wastes the overlap it just bought}{
  \p{With \code{-I IO_RUN_LATER}, a completed I/O does not immediately return the issuing process to the CPU; whatever was running keeps running. What happens with \code{./process-run.py -l 3:0,5:100,5:100,5:100 -S SWITCH_ON_IO -I IO_RUN_LATER -c -p}? Are system resources being used effectively?}
}{
  \prediction{They are not. All three CPU-bound processes finish before we return to the I/O-bound one, which then issues its next \code{io} with nothing left to overlap it against — the concurrency benefit evaporates.}
  \p{The trace is long, but the totals say it:}
  \codeblock{plaintext}{\verb<<<|
Stats: Total Time 31
Stats: CPU Busy 21 (67.74%)
Stats: IO Busy  15 (48.39%)
<<<}
}

\exercise{7}{IO_RUN_IMMEDIATE and why recent I/O predicts more I/O}{
  \p{Run the same processes with \code{-I IO_RUN_IMMEDIATE}, which runs the issuing process as soon as its I/O completes. How does the behavior differ, and why might rerunning a process that just finished I/O be a good idea?}
}{
  \prediction{Returning to the I/O-bound process lets it issue its next \code{io} before handing the CPU back, so the requests stay in flight alongside the compute. As a heuristic it is a bet that a process which just did I/O will do more of it.}
  \p{The bet pays:}
  \codeblock{plaintext}{\verb<<<|
Stats: Total Time 21
Stats: CPU Busy 21 (100.00%)
Stats: IO Busy  15 (71.43%)
<<<}
}

\exercise{8}{Which flag matters under randomly generated workloads}{
  \p{Run some randomly generated processes — \code{-s 1 -l 3:50,3:50}, \code{-s 2 -l 3:50,3:50}, \code{-s 3 -l 3:50,3:50}. Predict how the trace turns out. What changes between \code{-I IO_RUN_IMMEDIATE} and \code{-I IO_RUN_LATER}, and between \code{-S SWITCH_ON_IO} and \code{-S SWITCH_ON_END}?}
}{
  \prediction{With each instruction a coin flip between CPU and I/O, \code{IO_RUN_LATER} and \code{IO_RUN_IMMEDIATE} should be close to indistinguishable. \code{SWITCH_ON_IO} over \code{SWITCH_ON_END} should matter more and more as processes are added, since there is almost always CPU work waiting — though with only two processes the gain may be small, as both are likely to be blocked at some point.}
  \p{For seed \code{-s 1} the two \code{-I} policies perform identically, and enabling \code{SWITCH_ON_IO} improves both CPU and I/O utilization.}
}
}

\subtree[ostep-ch5]{
\title{Interlude: Process API}
\meta{number}{5}

\exercise{1}{fork() copies the address space, not the variable}{
  \p{Write a program that calls \code{fork()}. Before forking, set a variable — say \code{x} — to 100. What is its value in the child? What happens when parent and child both change it?}
}{
  \p{The child gets a copy of the parent's address space, #{x} included. Each subsequent write lands in that process's own copy, so neither sees the other's change.}
}

\exercise{2}{A file descriptor survives the fork}{
  \p{Open a file with \code{open()} and then \code{fork()}. Can both child and parent use the returned descriptor? What happens when they write to the file concurrently?}
}{
  \p{Both have access to the same descriptor. Concurrent writes can in principle interleave, but in my runs whichever process was scheduled first always finished writing before the other started — plausibly a scheduling artifact rather than a guarantee.}
}

\exercise{3}{Ordering child before parent without wait()}{
  \p{Write a program where the child prints "hello" and the parent prints "goodbye", with the child always printing first. Can you do it without calling \code{wait()} in the parent?}
}{
  \p{Reusing the shared file from the previous exercise: have the child write a value, and have the parent spin in a \code{while} loop until it reads that value back. It works, and it is miserable — which is presumably why \code{wait()} exists.}
}

\exercise{4}{What wait() returns, and what it does in the child}{
  \p{Now use \code{wait()} in the parent to wait for the child. What does it return? What happens if you call \code{wait()} in the child?}
}{
  \p{It returns the pid of the child that died. Called in the child — which has no children of its own — it returns #{-1} immediately.}
}

\exercise{5}{Why exec() has so many variants}{
  \p{Write a program that calls \code{fork()} and then some form of \code{exec()} to run \code{/bin/ls}. Try the variants — \code{execl()}, \code{execle()}, \code{execlp()}, \code{execv()}, \code{execvp()}, \code{execvP()}. Why are there so many spellings of one call?}
}{
  \p{The suffixes are a small matrix of three independent choices. The \code{v} family takes a variable-size argument vector where the \code{l} family takes a fixed argument list. The \code{e} family takes an explicit environment — the list of environment variables the new program will see — instead of inheriting the caller's. The \code{p} family resolves the program name against \code{PATH}, the way a shell does, instead of requiring a path.}
}

\exercise{6}{When waitpid() beats wait()}{
  \p{Rewrite the previous program using \code{waitpid()} instead of \code{wait()}. When is \code{waitpid()} the one you want?}
}{
  \p{When a parent has several children. \code{wait()} returns on whichever dies first; \code{waitpid()} lets you name the child you care about.}
}

\exercise{7}{printf() after closing STDOUT in the child}{
  \p{Create a child process that closes standard output (\code{STDOUT_FILENO}). What happens if the child then calls \code{printf()}?}
}{
  \p{Nothing prints from the child. The parent's stdout is a separate descriptor and is untouched, so it still prints.}
}

\exercise{8}{Connecting two children with pipe()}{
  \p{Create two children and connect the standard output of one to the standard input of the other using \code{pipe()}.}
}{
  \p{It worked only once I added \code{fflush()} — the writer's output was sitting in stdio's buffer rather than in the pipe. Whether that is required in general or an artifact of how I structured the writer, I am not sure.}
}
}

\subtree[ostep-ch7]{
\title{Scheduling: Introduction}
\meta{number}{7}

\exercise{1}{When SJF matches FIFO}{
  \p{For what types of workloads does [[VJ4F]] deliver the same turnaround times as [[JA6Z]]?}
}{
  \p{Workloads that arrive in increasing order of total job time — FIFO is then already running them shortest-first.}
}

\exercise{2}{When SJF matches round robin}{
  \p{For what workloads and quantum lengths does SJF deliver the same response times as [[NEL8]]?}
}{
  \p{Short jobs and long quanta. A quantum long enough to hold a whole job turns round robin into run-to-completion, and short jobs make the ordering hardly matter.}
}

\exercise{3}{Response time under SJF as jobs grow}{
  \p{What happens to response time under SJF as job lengths increase?}
}{
  \p{It degrades: the longest job waits out every shorter one ahead of it, so as those grow the response time grows with them.}
}

\exercise{4}{Worst-case response time under round robin}{
  \p{What happens to response time under round robin as the quantum grows? Can you write the worst-case response time for #{N} jobs?}
}{
  \p{Job #{i} waits out the #{i - 1} slices ahead of it, so with quantum #{S} the worst case is the last job's:}
  ##{\mathrm{RT}_{\max} = S(N - 1)}
  \p{and averaging over the #{N} jobs halves it,}
  ##{\overline{\mathrm{RT}} = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i = 1}^{N} (i - 1)S = \frac{S}{N} \cdot \frac{N(N-1)}{2} = \frac{S(N - 1)}{2}}
  \p{Both grow linearly in the quantum: round robin's responsiveness is precisely what a long quantum spends.}
}
}
